\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Month 1 \'97 Lab Architecture & Environment Setup\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Week 1: Hypervisor Deployment & Virtual Networking\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Stand up the hypervisor and the isolated virtual network that everything else plugs into.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Install VirtualBox (or VMware Workstation Player).\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Create a 
\f0\b Host-Only Network
\f1\b0  (VirtualBox: File 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  Host Network Manager) or 
\f0\b Internal Network
\f1\b0  \'97 internal networks are the more airtight choice since they have zero path to your host adapter.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Note the subnet VirtualBox assigns by default (commonly 
\f3\fs26 192.168.56.0/24
\f1\fs24 ) or set a custom one.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Document your topology: draw the network (host, Kali, Metasploitable), write down the subnet, and note which hypervisor network mode each VM's NIC will use.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  A named virtual network ready to attach VMs to, with a one-page diagram + addressing table saved for reference.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 2: Provisioning the Attacker Machine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Get Kali Linux running and attached to the isolated network only.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Download the official Kali Linux VM image (OVA) from kali.org.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Import into your hypervisor; 
\f0\b before first boot
\f1\b0 , set its network adapter to the internal/host-only network created in Week 1 \'97 not NAT or Bridged.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Boot, log in, run 
\f3\fs26 sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade -y
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Set a static IP inside the lab subnet (e.g. 
\f3\fs26 10.10.10.10
\f1\fs24  or the equivalent in your Week-1 subnet) via 
\f3\fs26 /etc/network/interfaces
\f1\fs24 or NetworkManager.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  Kali boots, is fully updated, and can be confirmed to have 
\f2\i no
\f1\i0  route out to the real internet (test with 
\f3\fs26 ping 8.8.8.8
\f1\fs24 \'97 it should fail; that's correct).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 3: Provisioning the Victim Machine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Get a deliberately vulnerable target running alongside Kali.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Download Metasploitable 2 (simpler, faster to start with) or Metasploitable 3 (more modern, harder).\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Import and attach its NIC to the same isolated network.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Boot it (default Metasploitable 2 creds: 
\f3\fs26 msfadmin
\f1\fs24  / 
\f3\fs26 msfadmin
\f1\fs24 ).\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Assign it a static IP in the same subnet as Kali.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Kali: 
\f3\fs26 ping <metasploitable-ip>
\f1\fs24  to confirm connectivity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  Two VMs on one isolated network that can reach each other and nothing else.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 4: Base Environment Verification\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Confirm the environment is sound and create a rollback point before anything gets broken.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Kali, run a basic scan: 
\f3\fs26 nmap -sV <metasploitable-ip>
\f1\fs24  to confirm services are visible.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Take 
\f0\b snapshots
\f1\b0  of both VMs in their clean state (hypervisor snapshot feature) \'97 label them clearly, e.g. 
\f3\fs26 clean-baseline
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Spend some time on basic Linux CLI fluency if you're newer to it: navigation (
\f3\fs26 cd
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 ls
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 pwd
\f1\fs24 ), file viewing (
\f3\fs26 cat
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 less
\f1\fs24 ), permissions (
\f3\fs26 chmod
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 sudo
\f1\fs24 ), and networking basics (
\f3\fs26 ip a
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 ping
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 ss -tulpn
\f1\fs24 ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  Verified connectivity, a recovery snapshot for both machines, and baseline command-line comfort before Month 2 ramps up.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 5. Month 2 \'97 Traffic Analysis & Attack Simulation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Week 5: Wireshark Installation & Interface Mastery\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Learn to read raw traffic before generating anything interesting to look at.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wireshark ships with Kali \'97 launch it (
\f3\fs26 sudo wireshark
\f1\fs24  or via GUI).\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Select the correct capture interface (the one attached to your lab network).\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Explore display filters (
\f3\fs26 ip.addr == <ip>
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 tcp.port == 80
\f1\fs24 ) and the default coloring rules (green = normal TCP, black = errors, etc.).\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Start a capture with both VMs idle, let it run a minute, stop it, and just look \'97 DNS chatter, ARP, any background noise.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  Comfortable navigating the Wireshark UI and reading a basic capture cold.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 6: Cleartext Protocol Analysis (FTP/HTTP)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  See firsthand why cleartext protocols are dangerous \'97 this is usually the "aha" moment of the whole project.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Start a Wireshark capture on Kali.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Kali, connect to Metasploitable's FTP service: 
\f3\fs26 ftp <metasploitable-ip>
\f1\fs24 , log in with a test credential.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stop the capture.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In Wireshark, right-click the FTP stream 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  
\f0\b Follow 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  TCP Stream
\f1\b0  \'97 you'll see the username and password in plain text.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Repeat with a basic HTTP login form if Metasploitable's web apps (e.g. DVWA, if installed) expose one.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  A saved 
\f3\fs26 .pcap
\f1\fs24  showing extracted cleartext credentials, and a clear understanding of why encrypted protocols (SFTP, HTTPS) exist.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 7: Port Scanning & Reconnaissance Footprints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Understand what a scan looks like from the network defender's side.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Start a Wireshark capture, then run a SYN scan from Kali: 
\f3\fs26 nmap -sS <metasploitable-ip>
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stop the capture and filter on 
\f3\fs26 tcp.flags.syn==1 && tcp.flags.ack==0
\f1\fs24  to isolate the scan's probe packets.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Identify the three-part patterns: SYN (probe) 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  SYN/ACK (open port) or RST (closed port).\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Note how many packets a single scan generates \'97 this is the "noise" an intrusion detection system would key on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  A pcap you can point to and explain, packet by packet, what a port scan looks like on the wire.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 8: Exploit Captures & Log Review\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Run one well-documented, contained exploit and see what it leaves behind on the network.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 With Wireshark capturing, use Metasploit Framework (
\f3\fs26 msfconsole
\f1\fs24 ) to run the classic 
\f0\b VSFTPD v2.3.4 backdoor
\f1\b0 exploit against Metasploitable 2 (
\f3\fs26 exploit/unix/ftp/vsftpd_234_backdoor
\f1\fs24 ) \'97 this is one of the most-taught, safest, fully self-contained exploits for learning, entirely within your isolated lab.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Confirm shell access on the target, then stop the capture.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Review the pcap: identify the exploit delivery, the resulting connection, and any anomalous traffic pattern (e.g. connection to a non-standard port) that a defender would flag.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  A full "attack story" told through a single pcap, from delivery to compromise \'97 this becomes the core evidence for your Month 3 firewall test.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 6. Month 3 \'97 Perimeter Defense & Network Segmentation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Week 9: pfSense Deployment & Interface Mapping\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Introduce a real firewall as the chokepoint between attacker and victim.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Download the pfSense (or OPNsense) ISO.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Create a new VM with 
\f0\b three virtual NICs
\f1\b0 .\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Install pfSense; during setup, assign: NIC1 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  WAN (lab-facing "internet" side), NIC2 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  LAN (management segment), NIC3 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  OPT1/DMZ.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Re-IP Kali onto the LAN segment and Metasploitable onto the DMZ segment per the addressing table in Section 2.2.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Outcome:
\f1\b0  pfSense boots, all three interfaces show correct IPs, and you can reach its web GUI from Kali.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Week 10: Firewall Policy Configuration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Objective:
\f1\b0  Move from "everything reachable" to "default deny."\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Log into the pfSense web portal (
\f3\fs26 https://10.10.10.1
\f1\fs24  from Kali, by default).\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Under Firewall 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  Rules, set the LAN and DMZ interfaces to 
\f0\b deny all inbound by default
\f1\b0 .\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Add narrow allow rules \'97 e.g. permit LAN 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  DMZ on port 21 (FTP) or 22 (SSH) only, matching what you actually need to test.\
